<section class="source-doc card" id="source-charter" data-source-id="charter">
  <div class="kicker">Source 01 · HTML source</div>
  <h2>Product charter</h2>
  <div class="source-file">generic-product-catalogue-charter-v0.1.html</div>
  <div class="source-content"><header><div class="topbar"><div class="brand"><strong>Generic Product Catalogue</strong><span>Data Product Charter v0.1 - Draft for discovery and validation</span></div><button type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print</button></div></header>
<div class="layout">
  
  <div class="source-main">
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Generic Product Catalogue</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Data Product Charter v0.1</p>
      <div class="doc-status"><span class="status draft">Draft for discovery</span><span class="status confirmed">Confirmed fact</span><span class="status proposed">Proposed</span><span class="status assumption">Assumption</span><span class="status decision">Decision required</span><span class="status evidence">Evidence required</span></div>
      <p>This charter defines the initial business promise, product boundary, consumers, outputs, service expectations, ownership model and validation agenda for a shared analytical product catalogue supporting Consumer Data Science Function.</p>
      <div class="callout warning"><strong>Approval status:</strong> this is not an approved charter. It is intentionally explicit about unresolved ownership, scope, source, grain, retention and service-level decisions.</div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-charter-definition">
      <h2>2. Product definition and business promise</h2>
      <h3>Product promise</h3>
      <div class="promise">Provide Consumer Data Science Function teams with a trusted, documented and historically queryable representation of product data, identifiers and hierarchy context so they can train, test and evaluate models without maintaining separate analytical product-data pipelines.</div>
      <h3>Product intent</h3>
      <p><span class="status confirmed">Confirmed</span> Create a unified, standardised analytical product catalogue for Consumer Team A, Consumer Team B and Consumer Team C, replacing the fragmented analytical approach in which each squad independently organises Product Aggregation Service and hierarchy data.</p>
      <h3>What makes this a data product</h3>
      <div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">Data product characteristics</div>
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  PRODUCT["Generic Product Catalogue"]
  REUSE["Reusable<br>Shared across three squads"]
  ACTIVE["Active<br>Reports freshness, quality and health"]
  STANDARD["Standardised<br>Canonical grains, IDs and contracts"]
  VALUE["Measurable value<br>Less duplication and faster modelling"]
  OWNED["Owned<br>Business and technical lifecycle accountability"]
  FAIR["FAIR<br>Findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable"]
  PRODUCT --&gt; REUSE
  PRODUCT --&gt; ACTIVE
  PRODUCT --&gt; STANDARD
  PRODUCT --&gt; VALUE
  PRODUCT --&gt; OWNED
  PRODUCT --&gt; FAIR
        </pre>
      </div>
      <h3>Product-level boundaries</h3>
      <ul><li>The product is a logical business and service construct, not a synonym for one table.</li><li>It may publish several datasets or views through separately contracted output ports.</li><li>It consumes producer-aligned product and hierarchy sources but is aligned to the consumer-analytics use case.</li><li>It is not intended to become part of the customer-facing real-time serving path in the initial scope.</li></ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-charter-problem">
      <h2>3. Problem, opportunity and strategic context</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="panel"><h3>Current problem</h3><ul><li>Product data is fragmented across Document Database, Relational Database, Search Platform and Analytics Platform.</li><li>Rich Product Aggregation Service data is not centrally available for analytical use in Analytics Platform.</li><li>Identifiers use inconsistent names, formats and leading-zero rules.</li><li>Data Scientists depend on squad-specific engineering for new attributes.</li><li>History, quality controls, monitoring and documentation are inconsistent.</li><li>Clickstream and transaction joins are fragile or poorly documented.</li></ul></div>
        <div class="panel"><h3>Opportunity</h3><ul><li>Reduce duplicate databases, pipelines and transformation logic.</li><li>Release Data Science capacity for model improvement.</li><li>Improve consistency of training and back-testing data.</li><li>Make new attributes discoverable and reusable.</li><li>Create a governed product with ownership, contracts and service expectations.</li><li>Provide a foundation reusable by future consumer analytics use cases.</li></ul></div>
      </div>
      <div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">Problem-to-value chain</div>
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  FRAG["Fragmented product data"] --&gt; DUP["Duplicate stores and logic"]
  DUP --&gt; FRICTION["Discovery and engineering friction"]
  FRICTION --&gt; COST["Reduced Data Science capacity"]
  FRICTION --&gt; RISK["Inconsistent model inputs"]
  PRODUCT["Shared governed data product"] --&gt; TRUST["Standard IDs, history and quality"]
  TRUST --&gt; ADOPT["Reusable consumer contracts"]
  ADOPT --&gt; SPEED["Faster modelling and experimentation"]
  ADOPT --&gt; CONSIST["Consistent training and evaluation"]
        </pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-charter-consumers">
      <h2>4. Consumers, personas and priority use cases</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Product charter - table 2"><thead><tr><th>Consumer</th><th>Primary use cases</th><th>Migration-critical needs</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Science Team A<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Role Holder A</span></td><td>Daily models, offline tests, feature engineering and text/query matching.</td><td>Broad and complete attributes, stable transaction and clickstream joins, discoverability and [agreed history period] of daily history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Science Team B<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Do Role Holder C</span></td><td>Model training, back-testing, feature engineering and reconstruction of what was active or in stock.</td><td>Product Aggregation Service richness without repeated engineering requests and at least [agreed history period] of history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Team C / Triggered Communications<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Role Holder B</span></td><td>Sub-category prediction, campaign-to-PLP mapping, paid-media and consumer analysis.</td><td>Product definition, business-unit context, navigation/breadcrumbs and the Consumer Team B requirement superset.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Engineering Function</td><td>Build, operate and evolve the product and its contracts.</td><td>Approved sources, platform pattern, ownership, quality policy and support model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Future analytical consumers</td><td><span class="status assumption">Potential reuse where the same business promise applies.</span></td><td>Must not dilute the initial analytics scope or introduce unapproved scope.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">Consumer-to-product value model</div>
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart TB
  PRODUCT["Generic Product Catalogue"]
  SEARCH["Consumer Team A"] --&gt;|"Query and clickstream features"| PRODUCT
  TEAM_B["Consumer Team B"] --&gt;|"Training and back-testing"| PRODUCT
  TEAM_C["Consumer Team C"] --&gt;|"Classification and navigation context"| PRODUCT
  PRODUCT --&gt; CURR["Trusted current product state"]
  PRODUCT --&gt; HIST["Point-in-time history"]
  PRODUCT --&gt; IDS["Canonical identifiers and joins"]
  PRODUCT --&gt; DISC["Discoverable attributes and metadata"]
  CURR --&gt; VALUE["Faster, more consistent model delivery"]
  HIST --&gt; VALUE
  IDS --&gt; VALUE
  DISC --&gt; VALUE
        </pre>
      </div>
      <h3>Priority business use cases</h3>
      <ol><li>Train models using consistent product and hierarchy features.</li><li>Back-test models against the product state applicable to a historical date.</li><li>Perform offline evaluation without depending on operational Document Database, Relational Database or Search Platform stores.</li><li>Join product data reliably to transactions and clickstream.</li><li>Discover and evaluate new Product Aggregation Service attributes.</li><li>Map products and campaigns to selling categories, PLPs and master breadcrumbs.</li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-charter-scope">
      <h2>5. Scope, exclusions and product boundary</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="panel"><h3>Proposed MVP scope</h3><ul><li>Canonical Product Information Platform, Article and SKU identifiers.</li><li>Raw and cleaned identifier representations.</li><li>Current Article-level consumer view.</li><li>Critical Product Aggregation Service attributes.</li><li>ERP buying hierarchy and Product Information Platform selling hierarchy context.</li><li>Category relationships and master breadcrumb.</li><li>Daily historical snapshot.</li><li>Identifier mapping output.</li><li>Automated quality, freshness and operational monitoring.</li><li>Data Catalogue documentation and governed access.</li></ul></div>
