    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-executive-summary">
      <h2>2. Executive summary</h2>
      <p class="lead">consumer analytics product data is fragmented across squad-owned operational and analytical stores. The proposed change is to provide a unified, standardised, historically queryable product data product in Analytics Platform for Data Science use, while leaving real-time customer-serving systems in place.</p>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="panel"><h3>Problem</h3><ul><li>Three squads solve materially similar product-data problems independently.</li><li>Rich Product Aggregation Service attributes are not available in Analytics Platform.</li><li>Identifiers, hierarchy joins, leading-zero handling, and field terminology are inconsistent.</li><li>Historical retention, documentation, quality controls, and monitoring are insufficient.</li></ul></div>
        <div class="panel"><h3>Required outcome</h3><ul><li>One discoverable analytical product catalogue for Consumer Team A, Consumer Team B, and Consumer Team C.</li><li>Standard identifiers and hierarchy context.</li><li>Daily history retained for at least [agreed history period], with a stated user need of two to [agreed history period].</li><li>Product Aggregation Service richness plus Analytics Platform analytical context.</li><li>Data quality, monitoring, ownership, and Data Catalogue documentation.</li></ul></div>
      </div>
      <h3>Architectural position</h3>
      <p><span class="tag proposed">Proposed</span> Treat the capability as a governed analytical data product rather than a replacement for Product Aggregation Service or the existing operational stores. Separate the batch analytical plane from the real-time serving plane, but define stable identifiers and contracts between them.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-drivers">
      <h2>3. Business drivers and intended outcomes</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Solution context - table 2">
        <thead><tr><th>Driver</th><th>Current impact</th><th>Intended outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Cost of additional databases</td><td>Separate Document Database and Relational Database implementations are maintained by consumer analytics squads.</td><td>Reduce squad-owned analytical duplication and concentrate shared capability in a governed platform.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duplicated engineering and Data Science effort</td><td>Each squad cleans, maps, snapshots, and interprets similar data independently.</td><td>Reusable product data and shared transformation logic.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Opportunity cost</td><td>Data Scientists spend time finding and organising data instead of advancing algorithms.</td><td>Faster experimentation, feature engineering, training, back-testing, and offline evaluation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inconsistency and fragility</td><td>Six or more identifier names, undocumented leading-zero rules, fragile clickstream joins, and squad-specific field subsets.</td><td>Authoritative terminology, standard identifiers, documented joins, and validated data contracts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Incomplete analytical coverage</td><td>Rich online attributes are held in Product Aggregation Service-derived stores but not Analytics Platform.</td><td>Broad Product Aggregation Service representation available for analytical discovery and modelling.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Control weakness</td><td>No Data Quality Framework checks on current consumer product-data pipelines; limited failure visibility.</td><td>Automated quality checks, observability, ownership, and operational support.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <h3>Success measures to define during HLD</h3>
      <ul><li>Reduction in squad-maintained analytical product pipelines and stores.</li><li>Percentage of agreed Product Aggregation Service fields available and documented.</li><li>Freshness and completeness service levels achieved.</li><li>Historical days available without gaps.</li><li>Model pipelines migrated and independently validated.</li><li>Mean time to detect and resolve failed or degraded loads.</li></ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-scope">
      <h2>4. Scope and boundaries</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="panel"><h3>In scope</h3><ul><li>Analytical product catalogue for the three Consumer Data Science Function squads.</li><li>Product attributes required for model training, back-testing, offline evaluation, feature engineering, and historical analysis.</li><li>Product identifiers and cross-system mappings.</li><li>Product Information Platform selling hierarchy and ERP buying hierarchy context.</li><li>Daily product snapshots and historical retention.</li><li>Category, navigation, and breadcrumb context.</li><li>Quality controls, pipeline monitoring, metadata, ownership, and Data Catalogue documentation.</li><li>Country-specific product context for Region A, Region B, and Region C where supplied.</li></ul></div>
        <div class="panel"><h3>Out of scope</h3><ul><li>Replacement of Product Aggregation Service or its real-time APIs.</li><li>Replacement of Document Database, Relational Database, or Search Platform for customer-facing real-time serving in the initial change.</li><li>Redesign of website, app, search, or recommendation serving paths.</li><li>Detailed physical Analytics Platform source-to-target mapping.</li><li>Unapproved assumption that every Product Aggregation Service field must become a first-class typed column.</li></ul></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Boundary principle:</strong> batch and historical Data Science use belongs in the unified analytical data product; real-time customer serving remains with the operational Product Aggregation Service ecosystem unless separately approved.</div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-stakeholders">
      <h2>5. Stakeholders, consumers, and use cases</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Solution context - table 3">
        <thead><tr><th>Stakeholder or consumer</th><th>Current use</th><th>Key need</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Science Team A<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Role Holder A</span></td><td>Uses Team B Document Database, Analytics Platform hierarchy data, and Search Platform; models run daily; text matching uses broad text coverage.</td><td>A complete central source, discoverable attributes, robust transaction/clickstream joins, and [agreed history period] of daily history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Science Team B<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Do Role Holder C</span></td><td>Maintains Document Database updated through batch and Event Streaming Platform flows; writes daily snapshots to Analytics Platform.</td><td>Everything needed from Product Aggregation Service without repeated engineering requests; at least [agreed history period] of history for availability-aware back-testing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Team C / Triggered Communications<br><span class="small">Lead referenced: Role Holder B</span></td><td>Uses Relational Database/Time-series Database sourced from Product Aggregation Service, plus daily exports to a Data Processing Platform Volume.</td><td>Product definition, navigation/breadcrumb mapping, business-unit context, and the superset of Consumer Team B requirements.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consumer Data Engineering Function</td><td>New squad expected to support consumer analytics.</td><td>Build and operate the unified data product, pipelines, tests, monitoring, and support model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Digital Product and Analytics Product Managers</td><td>Prioritise customer-experience and analytics work.</td><td>Clear scope, measurable outcomes, and governed requirements.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Web, App, and Search Engineering</td><td>Consume Product Aggregation Service for real-time product experiences.</td><td>Stable upstream behaviour; no disruption from analytical changes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Architecture, Data Governance, Information Security</td><td>Approve patterns, data movement, classification, access, and controls.</td><td>Explicit decisions, lineage, ownership, security boundaries, and evidence of compliance.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-current-state">
      <h2>6. Current-state architecture and problems</h2>
      <p>Product Information Platform is the ultimate product-content source, but the analytical and operational paths diverge. Product Aggregation Service receives enriched, decorated product data and combines it with six other API domains. Analytics Platform receives raw ERP, Product Lifecycle Platform, and Product Information Platform data through different pipelines. The two paths have not been connected, driving squad-specific workarounds.</p>
      <div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">Current-state logical data flow</div>
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  PRODUCT_INFO["Product Information Platform<br>Product content source"] --&gt; CONTENT_API["Product Content API"]
  ERP["ERP / Product Lifecycle Platform<br>Buying and merchandising data"] --&gt; ANALYTICS_PLATFORM["Analytics Platform<br>Existing analytical layers"]
  PRODUCT_INFO --&gt; ANALYTICS_PLATFORM
  CONTENT_API --&gt; GATEWAY["Product Aggregation Service<br>Aggregates seven upstream APIs"]
  STOCK_API["Stock API"] --&gt; GATEWAY
