    <section class="section" id="src-solution-context-identifiers">
      <h2>8. Product identifier and grain model</h2>
      <div class="callout warning"><strong>Design-critical point:</strong> several terms are overloaded. “Product”, “article”, “variant”, and “product colour” can refer to different grains in different systems. The HLD must define canonical names, data types, uniqueness rules, and valid time ranges.</div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Solution context - table 6"><thead><tr><th>Canonical concept</th><th>Common aliases</th><th>Grain</th><th>Rules and cautions</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Product Information Platform ID</td><td><code>productExternalId</code>, <code>externalProductId</code>, Product ID</td><td>Product or generic product</td><td>Usually begins with <code>P</code>, but display sets, product builder, and made-to-order products may use <code>ALT1</code>, <code>ALT2</code>, or <code>ALT3</code>. Do not derive semantics solely from the prefix.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Article ID</td><td>Generic Article, Product Variant ID, article ID, article number, product colour ID</td><td>Colour level; level 8 relationship in the hierarchy</td><td>Confirmed as the common working grain by all three squads and proposed as the standard analytical key. Leading-zero handling must be standardised.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SKU ID</td><td>Variant Article, product size, product colour-size, product variant number</td><td>Size / lowest online grain</td><td>Stock, price, and promotions are applied here; adding to basket uses the SKU ID.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UPC / EAN</td><td>Barcode, EAN8, EAN13</td><td>Barcode attached to a SKU or item</td><td>One SKU can have multiple barcodes. Legacy UPCs may be removed and recycled; they must not be the sole durable key.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>legacy grouping key</td><td>legacy grouping key code, department-legacy grouping key, TCode</td><td>Generic article grouping within a department for Category A &amp; Home</td><td>Not unique over time; a legacy grouping key can move departments and Category B may not use strokes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>internal_product_key</code></td><td>Analytics Platform internal product key</td><td>Analytics Platform row / surrogate key</td><td>Useful for internal warehouse and transaction joins, but inclusion in the new consumer contract is an architecture decision.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <div class="diagram"><div class="diagram-title">Conceptual product grain and identifier relationships</div>
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart TB
  PRODUCT_INFO["Product Information Platform product<br>standard / alternate type prefixes identifier"]
  ARTICLE1["Article / colour 1<br>Canonical Article ID"]
  ARTICLE2["Article / colour 2<br>Canonical Article ID"]
  SKU1["SKU / size 1<br>SKU ID"]
  SKU2["SKU / size 2<br>SKU ID"]
  SKU3["SKU / size 1<br>SKU ID"]
  BC1["UPC / EAN barcode(s)<br>Recyclable legacy identifiers"]
  BC2["UPC / EAN barcode(s)"]
  LEGACY_GROUP_KEY["legacy grouping key / department-legacy grouping key<br>Non-durable grouping"]
  DPK["internal_product_key<br>Analytics Platform surrogate mapping"]
  PRODUCT_INFO --&gt; ARTICLE1
  PRODUCT_INFO --&gt; ARTICLE2
  ARTICLE1 --&gt; SKU1
  ARTICLE1 --&gt; SKU2
  ARTICLE2 --&gt; SKU3
  SKU1 --&gt; BC1
  SKU2 --&gt; BC2
  LEGACY_GROUP_KEY -. "groups generic article" .-&gt; PRODUCT_INFO
