B model use cases, required fields and migration acceptance. The Product Owner must approve overall scope and the Product Aggregation Service owner must approve source semantics.”</p><h3>Part C — Business baseline</h3><h3>5. Business purpose and scope</h3><p>Please validate the following statements.</p><div class="table-label">Table 3</div><div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Stakeholder requirements baseline questionnaire - table 3"><tr><th><p><strong>ID</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Current understanding</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Status</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Correction or owner</strong></p></th></tr><tr><td><p>BR-01</p></td><td><p>Create one governed analytical product catalogue for Consumer Team A, Consumer Team B and Consumer Data Science Team C.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>BR-02</p></td><td><p>Reduce duplicated squad-owned product stores, pipelines and transformation rules.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>BR-03</p></td><td><p>Support training, feature engineering, back-testing, offline evaluation and historical analysis.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>BR-04</p></td><td><p>Provide standard identifiers, hierarchies, discoverable attributes, history and quality evidence.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>BR-05</p></td><td><p>The initial scope is analytical. Replacement of real-time Product Aggregation Service APIs and serving stores is out of scope.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>BR-06</p></td><td><p>The data product may publish several contracted outputs rather than one physical table.</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr></table></div><h4>Question 5.1 — Are any consumers, processes or countries missing from the scope?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Search, Consumer Team B and Consumer Team C are the initial consumers. Region B must be included in release one because the Consumer Team B models run separately for Region A and Region B. Region C coverage may follow after its source availability is confirmed.”</p><h4>Question 5.2 — What must explicitly remain out of scope for the first release?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Replacing Search Platform, Document Database or Relational Database in customer-facing serving paths is out of scope. Real-time feature serving, customer data and model deployment are also excluded.”</p><h4>Question 5.3 — How should success be measured?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Success means all three teams have migrated at least one production pipeline, three duplicated analytical product pipelines can be retired, agreed daily data is available before [organisation-specific time] on 99% of scheduled days, and critical identifier mappings achieve at least 99.9% coverage.”</p><h3>Part D — Consumer requirements</h3><h3>6. Consumer Data Science Team A</h3><h4>Current understanding</h4><p>Consumer Team A currently uses Team B Document Database, Analytics Platform hierarchy data and Search Platform. Models run daily. The team requires broad text coverage, reliable transaction and clickstream joins and potentially [agreed history period] of daily history.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> [Confirmed/Change required/Unresolved/Not applicable]</p><p><strong>Corrections:</strong> [Enter response]</p><h4>Priority use cases</h4><div class="table-label">Table 4</div><div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Stakeholder requirements baseline questionnaire - table 4"><tr><th><p><strong>Priority</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Use case or model</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Outcome supported</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Current data</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Owner</strong></p></th></tr><tr><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>2</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>3</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr></table></div><h4>Question 6.1 — What makes each use case successful?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Our offline search evaluation must find the correct Article for at least 99.5% of test clickstream records. Product names, descriptions, brand, colour, composition and category breadcrumbs must be populated consistently.”</p><h4>Question 6.2 — What product grain and joins are required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“The main view should use Article ID because colour affects search relevance. We also need Article-to-SKU mappings and a documented join from clickstream product ID plus colour name to Article ID.”</p><h4>Question 6.3 — What data timing is required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“The latest complete product dataset must be available by [organisation-specific time] [organisation-specific timezone] every day. [agreed history period] of daily history is required for evaluation and seasonality analysis.”</p><h4>Question 6.4 — How will Consumer Team A accept the new product?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“We will run the current and new datasets in parallel for four weeks. Acceptance requires agreed field parity, at least 99.5% clickstream join coverage and no material deterioration in offline search metrics.”</p><h3>7. Consumer Data Science Team B</h3><h4>Current understanding</h4><p>Consumer Team B maintains a Product Aggregation Service-derived Document Database with batch and Event Streaming Platform updates and writes daily snapshots to Analytics Platform. At least [agreed history period] of availability-aware history is required for training and back-testing.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> [Confirmed/Change required/Unresolved/Not applicable]</p><p><strong>Corrections:</strong> [Enter response]</p><h4>Priority use cases</h4><div class="table-label">Table 5</div><div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Stakeholder requirements baseline questionnaire - table 5"><tr><th><p><strong>Priority</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Use case or model</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Outcome supported</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Current data</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Owner</strong></p></th></tr><tr><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>2</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>3</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr></table></div><h4>Question 7.1 — What makes each use case successful?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“A recommendation back-test must use only products that were saleable in the relevant country on the historical evaluation date. Product similarity features must have consistent definitions across training runs.”</p><h4>Question 7.2 — What product grain and joins are required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Article is the principal recommendation grain. SKU-level availability must be aggregated using an agreed rule, such as an Article being available when at least one associated SKU is saleable.”</p><h4>Question 7.3 — What data timing is required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“The daily dataset must be published before the [organisation-specific time] model run. We need a minimum of [agreed history period] of daily history and must be able to reproduce the exact catalogue version used by a previous model.”</p><h4>Question 7.4 — How will Consumer Team B accept the new product?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“We will compare the new product with the existing Document Database snapshot for six weeks. Acceptance requires at least 99.9% Article coverage, agreed availability parity and statistically equivalent model results.”</p><h3>8. Consumer Team C and Triggered Communications Data Science</h3><h4>Current understanding</h4><p>Consumer Team C uses Product Aggregation Service-derived Relational Database/Time-series Database data and a daily Volume export. Product definition, navigation, breadcrumbs and business-unit context are important. Its requirements may be a superset of Consumer Team B requirements.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> [Confirmed/Change required/Unresolved/Not applicable]</p><p><strong>Corrections:</strong> [Enter response]</p><h4>Priority use cases</h4><div class="table-label">Table 6</div><div class="table-wrap"><table aria-label="Stakeholder requirements baseline questionnaire - table 6"><tr><th><p><strong>Priority</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Use case or model</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Outcome supported</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Current data</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Owner</strong></p></th></tr><tr><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>2</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>3</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td><td><p>[Enter]</p></td></tr></table></div><h4>Question 8.1 — What makes each use case successful?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Campaign selection must consistently identify the correct Article, navigation category, breadcrumb and business unit. Products that are not saleable in the target country must not enter eligible campaign populations.”</p><h4>Question 8.2 — What product grain and joins are required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Campaign selection begins at Article grain, but eligibility depends on SKU-country saleability. Products may belong to several categories, so we need a many-to-many category relationship and a documented master breadcrumb.”</p><h4>Question 8.3 — What data timing is required?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Daily product data must be ready by [organisation-specific time]. [agreed history period] of history is sufficient for campaign analysis, but current saleability must reflect the agreed overnight cut-off.”</p><h4>Question 8.4 — How will Consumer Team C accept the new product?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“We will compare campaign populations produced by the new catalogue with the current Relational Database process for four campaign cycles. Differences must be explained and approved before migration.”</p><h3>Part E — Data semantics</h3><h3>9. Product grain and identifier model</h3><pre class="mermaid attached-diagram" data-attachment-source="pasted-text.txt">flowchart TB
