isation-specific time]. A delay of up to [agreed delay tolerance] is tolerated before an incident is raised.”</p><h4>Question 11.2 — What does “as of a date” mean?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“For model back-testing, ‘as of [Project date removed]’ means the product facts and relationships that were valid at the end of [Project date removed], using only information available before the next daily publication.”</p><h4>Question 11.3 — How should late or corrected data be handled?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Changes arriving within seven days should restate the affected business dates. Restated dates must be marked, consumers notified and the previous published version retained for reproducibility.”</p><h4>Question 11.4 — How should deleted or discontinued products appear?</h4><p><strong>Your answer:</strong></p><p>[Enter response]</p><p>Illustrative example - replace or confirm</p><p>“Deleted products should disappear from the current active view but remain in history. The historical record should show the final active date and deletion or discontinuation reason where supplied.”</p><h3>Part H — Quality and publication controls</h3><h3>12. Data-quality requirements</h3><pre class="mermaid attached-diagram" data-attachment-source="pasted-text.txt">flowchart LR
